Hash eval cases in round-robin shards (applied by relative_oracle.py after A0)

A0's --write-dev-code-hashes split the cases into contiguous blocks, one per
shard. The expensive "issue 11698" cases sit together at the end of the
corpus, so the last shard took 957 s while the other 15 took 288-322 s.
Shard k now takes cases k, k + n, k + 2n, ..., and the parent puts the lines
back in case order. Measured on 8e9e512e0f: 474 s instead of 957 s, output
byte-identical (also at 1, 3 and 7 shards on a 26-case subset); peak memory
13.8 GB instead of 9.0 GB, since the heavy cases now run at the same time.

